// New articles, 17 November 2026 (part 2): filling thinner subjects — arts, science, space, Earth, technology and Oceania.
ADDART({id:"violin",t:"The Violin and Its Family",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎻",w:"Violin",r:["orchestra","musicw","guitar","mozart","opera"],
p:[["A singing instrument","The violin is a small stringed instrument played with a bow."],["A family","The violin family includes the viola, cello and double bass."],["Legendary makers","Violins by Stradivari are among the most valuable instruments ever made."]],
l:"The violin is a stringed instrument with four strings tuned in fifths, usually played by drawing a bow strung with horsehair across the strings, or by plucking them. It is the smallest and highest-pitched member of the violin family, which also includes the viola, the cello and the double bass, and it forms the largest section of the symphony orchestra. The violin developed in northern Italy in the early 16th century; the oldest surviving examples were made by Andrea Amati in Cremona around the 1550s–60s. Later Cremona makers, especially Antonio Stradivari and Giuseppe Guarneri ‘del Gesù’, made instruments in the 17th and 18th centuries that are still prized by top performers and can sell for millions of dollars. The violin is used in classical music, folk traditions from Ireland to India, country fiddling, jazz and pop. Famous violinists include Niccolò Paganini, whose dazzling technique made him a 19th-century celebrity.",
s:[["How it works","A violin’s body is a hollow box usually made of spruce for the top and maple for the back and sides. When the bow makes a string vibrate, the vibrations pass through a small wooden bridge to the body, which amplifies the sound. Inside, a thin ‘sound post’ and ‘bass bar’ shape the tone. Players change pitch by pressing strings against the fingerboard, which has no frets."],["The Cremona makers","Andrea Amati founded the Cremona tradition, followed by his grandson Nicolò Amati, who taught Antonio Stradivari. Stradivari made about 1,100 instruments in his long life, of which around 650 survive. Scientists have studied the wood, varnish and shape of these instruments, but blind tests suggest that many players cannot reliably tell them from the best modern violins."],["The family","The viola is slightly larger and lower than the violin. The cello is played sitting down, held between the knees, and the double bass, the largest, is played standing or on a tall stool. String quartets, made of two violins, a viola and a cello, became a favorite form for composers such as Haydn, Mozart and Beethoven."],["Fiddle traditions","‘Fiddle’ is another name for the violin, especially in folk music. Irish, Scottish, Scandinavian, Appalachian and Cajun fiddlers play lively dance tunes. In South India, the violin was adopted into Carnatic classical music in the 18th century and is played sitting on the floor."]],
k:[["Strings","Four, tuned G, D, A, E"],["Family","Violin, viola, cello, double bass"],["Origin","Northern Italy, early 1500s"],["Most famous maker","Antonio Stradivari (c. 1644–1737)"],["Bow hair","Horsehair"]],
d:[["c. 1530","Violins appear in northern Italy"],["c. 1560","Andrea Amati makes the oldest surviving violins"],["1700–1720","Stradivari’s ‘golden period’"],["1820","Paganini’s 24 Caprices published"],["2011","A Stradivari violin sells for about 15.9 million dollars"]],
s2:[["The bow","Modern bows, perfected by the French maker François Tourte around 1780s, curve inward and are made of pernambuco wood strung with about 150–200 horsehairs. Players rub the hair with rosin, a sticky resin, so it grips the strings."],["Paganini","Niccolò Paganini (1782–1840) played with such speed and showmanship that rumors claimed he had sold his soul to the devil. His 24 Caprices for solo violin remain a test for virtuosos."]],
sp:["The ‘Messiah’ Stradivarius","The ‘Messiah’ Stradivarius, made in 1716, is kept in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford and has hardly ever been played, so it looks almost new. Its condition helps researchers understand how Stradivari’s instruments originally appeared."],
g:[["Bow","A stick strung with horsehair, used to play strings"],["Bridge","The wooden piece that holds strings above the body"],["Fiddle","Another name for the violin, especially in folk music"],["Virtuoso","A highly skilled performer"]],
f:["About 650 Stradivari instruments survive.","A violin has no frets.","The violin section is the largest in an orchestra."],
fun:"Violin strings were traditionally made from sheep gut, and some players still prefer gut strings for their warm sound."});

ADDART({id:"piano",t:"The Piano",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎹",w:"Piano",r:["musicw","mozart","orchestra","jazz","violin"],
p:[["Soft and loud","The piano is a keyboard instrument whose hammers strike strings."],["Invented in Italy","Bartolomeo Cristofori built the first pianos around 1700."],["88 keys","A modern piano has 88 keys covering more than seven octaves."]],
l:"The piano is a keyboard instrument in which pressing a key makes a felt-covered hammer strike one or more metal strings, which vibrate to produce sound. Unlike the earlier harpsichord, which plucked its strings and could not change volume, the piano can play softly or loudly depending on how hard the keys are pressed, which gave it its original name, gravicembalo col piano e forte, ‘harpsichord with soft and loud’. It was invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori in Florence around 1700. Over the next 150 years, makers added iron frames, more keys and stronger strings, and the modern piano has 88 keys, about 230 strings under enormous tension and thousands of parts. Grand pianos have horizontal strings; upright pianos have vertical strings to save space. The piano became central to classical music, from Mozart, Beethoven and Chopin to Debussy, and to jazz, blues, pop and film music, and digital pianos now bring it into many homes.",
s:[["Invention","Cristofori, an instrument keeper for the Medici family, created an action, the mechanism linking key to hammer, that let the hammer strike the string and fall back immediately. Only three of his pianos survive, in New York, Rome and Leipzig."],["Development","Early pianos, called fortepianos, had wooden frames and a lighter sound. In the 19th century, cast-iron frames allowed thicker strings under greater tension, giving a louder, richer tone suited to concert halls. Steinway & Sons, founded in New York in 1853, and other firms developed the modern grand piano."],["How it works","Each key is a lever. When pressed, it throws a hammer at the strings and lifts a damper so the strings can ring. When released, the damper stops the sound. Most pianos have pedals: the right ‘sustain’ pedal lifts all dampers, and the left ‘soft’ pedal makes the sound quieter."],["The piano in music","Mozart and Beethoven wrote many piano sonatas and concertos, while Chopin and Liszt made the piano the star of Romantic music. Ragtime, jazz and blues pianists such as Scott Joplin and Duke Ellington created new styles. Upright pianos were once a common feature of family homes, used for entertainment before radio and recorded music."]],
k:[["Inventor","Bartolomeo Cristofori"],["Invented","About 1700, Florence"],["Keys","88"],["Strings","About 230"],["Main types","Grand and upright"]],
d:[["c. 1700","Cristofori builds the first piano"],["1770s","Mozart begins writing for the fortepiano"],["1825","Cast-iron frames introduced"],["1853","Steinway & Sons founded"],["1880s","88 keys become standard"],["1980s","Digital pianos spread"]],
s2:[["Tuning","The strings of a concert grand pull with a combined force of around 20 metric tons. Pianos need regular tuning because strings slowly stretch and wood responds to humidity. Piano tuners adjust each string with a special wrench."],["Player pianos","Player pianos, popular around 1900–1930, used rolls of punched paper to play music automatically. They were an early form of recorded music, and some rolls preserve the playing of famous composers."]],
sp:["The black and white keys","The pattern of 7 white and 5 black keys in each octave comes from Western scales. The white keys play the natural notes, and the black keys play sharps and flats. On some old harpsichords and pianos, the colors were reversed."],
g:[["Action","The mechanism that makes the hammer strike the string"],["Octave","The distance between one note and the next note of the same name"],["Damper","A felt pad that stops a string vibrating"],["Fortepiano","An early type of piano"]],
f:["The word piano is short for pianoforte, ‘soft-loud’.","A modern piano has about 230 strings.","Only three of Cristofori’s pianos survive."],
fun:"A concert grand piano contains around 12,000 parts, many of them in the action that connects each key to its hammer."});

ADDART({id:"mosaic",t:"Mosaics",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🧩",w:"Mosaic",r:["byzant","islamicart","rome","hagiasophia","pompeii","tessellations","fresco"],
p:[["Pictures from pieces","A mosaic is a picture or pattern made from small pieces of stone, glass or tile."],["Ancient art","Mosaics decorated Greek and Roman floors and Byzantine churches."],["Still made today","Artists still create mosaics for buildings, parks and streets."]],
l:"A mosaic is a picture or decorative pattern made by setting small pieces of colored stone, glass, ceramic, shell or other materials, called tesserae, into a surface such as a floor, wall or ceiling. Some of the earliest mosaics were made in Mesopotamia around 3000 BC using colored clay cones. The ancient Greeks made pebble mosaics, and the Romans decorated floors throughout their empire with mosaics of gods, animals, hunting scenes and geometric patterns; the famous Alexander Mosaic from Pompeii shows Alexander the Great in battle. In the Byzantine Empire, artists covered church walls and domes with shimmering gold and glass mosaics, as in Ravenna, Italy, and Hagia Sophia in Istanbul. Islamic art developed intricate geometric tile work, and the Aztecs made masks covered in turquoise. In the 20th century, the architect Antoni Gaudí used broken tiles in Barcelona, and today mosaics brighten subway stations, parks and public buildings around the world.",
s:[["How mosaics are made","Artists cut stone or glass into small pieces, often with special hammers and cutters, and set them into wet mortar or glue, close together. Gaps are filled with grout. Mosaicists can place pieces at slight angles so that light reflects in different directions, making surfaces glitter."],["Roman mosaics","Wealthy Romans paved their houses with mosaics, sometimes signed by the artists. The Alexander Mosaic, made around 100 BC for the House of the Faun in Pompeii, used millions of tiny tesserae. Roman villas in Britain, North Africa and Sicily, such as the Villa Romana del Casale, have spectacular floors."],["Byzantine splendor","Byzantine artists used glass tesserae backed with gold leaf. In the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna, completed in 547, mosaics show the emperor Justinian and empress Theodora with their courts. Gold backgrounds made churches seem filled with heavenly light."],["Modern mosaics","Gaudí’s Park Güell in Barcelona uses trencadís, a technique of covering curved surfaces with broken tiles and plates. Mexican muralists created huge mosaic facades, such as the library of the National Autonomous University of Mexico. Street artists, such as the French artist known as Invader, place pixel-like mosaics on walls in cities around the world."]],
k:[["Pieces called","Tesserae"],["Earliest examples","Mesopotamia, about 3000 BC"],["Famous Roman mosaic","Alexander Mosaic, Pompeii (c. 100 BC)"],["Famous Byzantine site","Ravenna, Italy"],["Modern technique","Trencadís (broken tiles)"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Clay cone mosaics in Uruk, Mesopotamia"],["c. 100 BC","Alexander Mosaic made in Pompeii"],["537","Hagia Sophia dedicated, later decorated with mosaics"],["547","San Vitale in Ravenna completed"],["1900–1914","Gaudí builds Park Güell"],["1956","Juan O’Gorman completes the UNAM library mosaic in Mexico City"]],
s2:[["Islamic tile work","In Morocco and Spain, zellij is a mosaic of hand-cut glazed tiles forming complex geometric stars. Persian and Central Asian mosques are covered with colorful tile mosaics. These designs follow mathematical rules of symmetry."],["Restoring mosaics","Conservators carefully clean ancient mosaics, reattach loose tesserae and sometimes lift whole floors to protect them. Many Roman mosaics were found by farmers plowing fields or builders digging foundations."]],
sp:["The Alexander Mosaic","The Alexander Mosaic shows Alexander the Great charging at the Persian king Darius III, probably at the Battle of Issus in 333 BC. It is thought to copy a lost Greek painting. Today it is in the National Archaeological Museum in Naples."],
g:[["Mosaic","An image made from small pieces of material"],["Tesserae","The small pieces used in a mosaic"],["Grout","Paste that fills gaps between tiles"],["Byzantine","Relating to the Eastern Roman Empire"]],
f:["The small pieces in a mosaic are called tesserae.","Byzantine mosaics often used real gold leaf.","Gaudí covered Park Güell with broken tiles."],
fun:"The French street artist Invader has placed thousands of small tile mosaics of 1970s video game aliens on walls around the world, and even one on the International Space Station."});

ADDART({id:"worlddance",t:"Dance Around the World",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"💃",w:"Dance",r:["ballet","phdance","indianmusic","musicals","kpop","folkmusic","flamenco","tango","breakdancing"],
p:[["Moving to music","Dance is the art of moving the body rhythmically, usually to music."],["Every culture dances","Dance is used for celebration, worship, storytelling and courtship."],["Living heritage","UNESCO protects dances such as tango, flamenco and capoeira."]],
l:"Dance is the art of moving the body in rhythmic and expressive ways, usually to music. Every known human culture dances, for celebration, worship, storytelling, courtship, exercise and social connection. Some dances are performed by trained professionals on stage, such as ballet, Indian classical dance and contemporary dance; others are social or folk dances that anyone can join, such as Irish ceili dances, the Latin American salsa and Filipino tinikling. Many dances carry deep cultural meaning: the Māori haka expresses identity and strength, the hula preserves Hawaiian history and religion, and flamenco expresses the passion of Andalusia’s Romani communities. UNESCO lists dances including Argentine tango (2009), flamenco (2010) and Brazilian capoeira (2014) as Intangible Cultural Heritage. In the 20th and 21st centuries, dance styles spread worldwide through film, television and social media, from swing and disco to breakdancing, which appeared at the 2024 Olympics, and K-pop dance routines.",
s:[["Ritual and storytelling","Cave paintings and ancient art show people dancing thousands of years ago. In many cultures, dances honor gods, mark harvests, heal the sick or prepare for war. Indian classical dances such as Bharatanatyam and Kathak tell religious stories through precise hand gestures, eye movements and footwork."],["Social dances","European court dances like the minuet gave way to the waltz in the 19th century, then shocking because partners held each other closely. The tango emerged in the dance halls of Buenos Aires and Montevideo in the late 1800s, and swing dances such as the Lindy Hop grew from African American communities in the 1920s–30s."],["Dances of identity","The haka is a Māori posture dance with chanting, foot-stamping and fierce expressions, performed at ceremonies and famously by New Zealand’s rugby team. Capoeira combines martial arts, acrobatics and music and was developed by enslaved Africans in Brazil. Flamenco combines song, guitar, clapping and dance."],["Dance today","Hip-hop dance styles, including breaking, popping and locking, began in New York and California in the 1970s. Breaking made its Olympic debut in Paris in 2024. Social media challenges spread dance moves around the world in days, and K-pop groups are famous for complex, synchronized choreography."]],
k:[["UNESCO-listed examples","Tango (2009), flamenco (2010), capoeira (2014)"],["Oldest evidence","Prehistoric rock art"],["Olympic debut of breaking","Paris 2024"],["Famous ceremonial dance","Haka (New Zealand)"],["International Dance Day","29 April"]],
d:[["c. 1820","The famous haka Ka Mate is composed"],["1880s","Tango develops in Buenos Aires and Montevideo"],["1920s","Lindy Hop emerges in Harlem"],["1970s","Hip-hop dance begins in New York"],["1994","Riverdance performed at Eurovision"],["2024","Breaking at the Paris Olympics"]],
s2:[["Why do we dance?","Scientists have found that moving in time with others increases feelings of closeness and trust and releases feel-good chemicals. Babies naturally bounce to music, suggesting that the urge to move to a beat is deeply human."],["Riverdance","A seven-minute performance of Irish dance during the 1994 Eurovision Song Contest interval became a worldwide phenomenon. The full Riverdance show has since been seen by tens of millions of people and revived interest in Irish step dancing."]],
sp:["Dances in many lands","Filipino tinikling dancers step between clapping bamboo poles; Chinese lion dancers perform at Lunar New Year; Greek dancers form lines in the syrtaki; and Ghanaian adowa dancers use graceful hand movements to communicate. Each reflects its people’s history, music and values."],
g:[["Choreography","The planned sequence of steps and movements in a dance"],["Folk dance","A traditional dance of a people or region"],["Intangible heritage","Living traditions, like dance and music, protected by UNESCO"],["Haka","A Māori ceremonial dance with chanting"]],
f:["Every known human culture dances.","The waltz was once considered shocking.","Breaking appeared at the Olympics for the first time in 2024."],
fun:"International Dance Day, 29 April, is the birthday of Jean-Georges Noverre, an 18th-century French dancer who helped create modern ballet."});

ADDART({id:"encyclopedias",t:"Encyclopedias",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📚",w:"Encyclopedia",r:["libraries","print","internet","enlightenment","novel"],
p:[["All knowledge in one place","An encyclopedia is a reference work that summarizes knowledge on many subjects."],["Ancient and modern","From Pliny’s Natural History to Wikipedia, people have tried to gather all knowledge."],["From books to screens","Most encyclopedias are now online rather than printed."]],
l:"An encyclopedia is a reference work containing articles that summarize knowledge, either on all subjects or on a particular field, usually arranged alphabetically or by topic. The word comes from Greek words meaning ‘general education’. One of the earliest surviving encyclopedic works is the Roman writer Pliny the Elder’s Natural History, completed in 77 AD. China’s Yongle Encyclopedia, finished in 1408, filled more than 11,000 volumes. In the 18th century, Denis Diderot and Jean le Rond d’Alembert’s French Encyclopédie spread Enlightenment ideas, while the Encyclopaedia Britannica was first published in Edinburgh in 1768–71. In the 20th century, families bought multi-volume printed sets, and in the 1990s encyclopedias moved to CD-ROMs. Wikipedia, launched in 2001 as a free encyclopedia anyone can edit, grew to millions of articles in hundreds of languages, and Britannica stopped printing in 2012. This encyclopedia itself belongs to this long tradition of organizing knowledge for readers.",
s:[["Ancient and medieval encyclopedias","Pliny’s Natural History, in 37 books, covered astronomy, geography, animals, plants, medicine and art. In the 7th century, Isidore of Seville compiled the Etymologies, a summary of knowledge used in Europe for centuries. Chinese scholars compiled huge leishu, collections of writings arranged by subject."],["The Yongle Encyclopedia","Commissioned by the Yongle Emperor of the Ming dynasty, it involved more than 2,000 scholars and copied texts on every subject into 11,095 volumes. It was too large to print, and most volumes were lost over the centuries; fewer than 4 percent survive."],["The Enlightenment","Diderot’s Encyclopédie (1751–1772), in 28 volumes, included articles on crafts and technology as well as philosophy, and some articles challenged the authority of the Church and king, leading to bans. Britannica, started by Scottish printers, became the best-known English-language encyclopedia."],["The digital age","Microsoft’s Encarta (1993–2009) put an encyclopedia on CD-ROM. Wikipedia, founded by Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger on 15 January 2001, relies on volunteer editors and citations to reliable sources. Studies have found its accuracy on many topics comparable to traditional encyclopedias, though errors and vandalism occur. Britannica published its last printed edition in 2010 and announced the end of printing in 2012."]],
k:[["Word origin","Greek for ‘general education’"],["Early example","Pliny’s Natural History (77 AD)"],["Largest historical encyclopedia","Yongle Encyclopedia (1408), 11,095 volumes"],["Britannica first published","1768–1771, Edinburgh"],["Wikipedia launched","15 January 2001"]],
d:[["77","Pliny completes his Natural History"],["c. 630","Isidore of Seville writes the Etymologies"],["1408","Yongle Encyclopedia completed"],["1751","Diderot’s Encyclopédie begins"],["1768","Encyclopaedia Britannica begins publication"],["1993","Encarta released on CD-ROM"],["2001","Wikipedia launched"],["2012","Britannica ends its print edition"]],
s2:[["How encyclopedias are written","Traditional encyclopedias hire experts and editors to write and check articles. Wikipedia relies on volunteers who must cite published sources and follow rules about neutrality. Both aim to summarize existing knowledge rather than publish new research or personal opinions."],["Using encyclopedias well","Encyclopedias are a good starting point for learning about a topic. For school work and research, readers are encouraged to follow the references to original sources, check several sources and note when information was last updated."]],
sp:["Pliny and the volcano","Pliny the Elder, author of the Natural History, was commander of a Roman fleet when Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 AD. He sailed toward the eruption to observe it and rescue people and died on the shore, probably from the fumes."],
g:[["Encyclopedia","A reference work that summarizes knowledge"],["Reference work","A book or resource for looking up information"],["Citation","A note showing where information came from"],["Edition","A version of a published work"]],
f:["The Yongle Encyclopedia filled more than 11,000 volumes.","Pliny the Elder died during the eruption of Vesuvius.","Britannica stopped printing in 2012 after 244 years."],
fun:"The first edition of the Encyclopaedia Britannica described California as a large country of the West Indies, a reminder of how much knowledge has grown since 1771."});

ADDART({id:"ciphers",t:"Codes and Ciphers",c:"Mathematics",i:"🔐",w:"Cipher",r:["cybersecurity","binary","turing","ww2","prime"],
p:[["Secret messages","Codes and ciphers turn messages into secret forms that only intended readers can understand."],["Code-breaking changed history","Breaking enemy codes helped win battles and wars."],["Protecting the internet","Modern encryption keeps online shopping, banking and messages safe."]],
l:"Codes and ciphers are methods of disguising messages so that only the intended reader can understand them. In everyday speech the words are used interchangeably, but strictly a code replaces whole words or phrases with other words or symbols, using a codebook, while a cipher changes individual letters or bits using a rule called an algorithm and a secret key. The study of making and breaking them is called cryptography and cryptanalysis. Julius Caesar shifted letters of the alphabet to hide military messages, Arab scholars invented frequency analysis to break such ciphers in the 9th century, and cipher machines like Germany’s Enigma were cracked by Polish and British mathematicians during World War II, including Alan Turing at Bletchley Park. In 1976–77, public-key cryptography made it possible to communicate securely with strangers, and it now protects internet banking, shopping and messaging. Mathematics, especially prime numbers, lies at the heart of modern encryption.",
s:[["Early ciphers","In the Caesar cipher, each letter is replaced by one a fixed number of places further along the alphabet, so with a shift of three, A becomes D. The Spartans used a scytale, a rod around which a strip of leather was wound to write a message. Substitution ciphers like these were easy to use but could be broken."],["Frequency analysis","Around 850 AD, the Arab scholar al-Kindi described how to break substitution ciphers by counting how often each symbol appears and comparing with common letters in the language, such as E in English. In 1586, deciphered letters revealed that Mary, Queen of Scots, had approved a plot to kill Elizabeth I, leading to her execution."],["Code-breaking in war","In 1917, British code-breakers decoded the Zimmermann Telegram, in which Germany proposed an alliance with Mexico against the United States, helping bring the US into World War I. In World War II, the breaking of Enigma and other codes at Bletchley Park gave the Allies vital intelligence. The US Marine Corps used Navajo speakers as code talkers, whose code was never broken."],["Modern cryptography","Today’s encryption uses complex mathematics. In public-key systems like RSA, created in 1977, anyone can use a public key to lock a message, but only the holder of a matching private key can unlock it; breaking it would mean factoring enormous numbers into primes. Researchers are developing new ‘post-quantum’ methods because future quantum computers might break current systems."]],
k:[["Code","Replaces words or phrases"],["Cipher","Changes letters or bits using a key"],["Frequency analysis","Described by al-Kindi, c. 850"],["Enigma","German WWII cipher machine, broken by the Allies"],["Public-key cryptography","1976–77"]],
d:[["c. 50 BC","Julius Caesar uses a shift cipher"],["c. 850","Al-Kindi describes frequency analysis"],["1586","Deciphered letters condemn Mary, Queen of Scots"],["1917","Zimmermann Telegram decoded"],["1932","Polish mathematicians break Enigma"],["1977","RSA encryption invented"],["2024","First post-quantum encryption standards published"]],
s2:[["The Voynich manuscript","The Voynich manuscript is a 15th-century book written in an unknown script, with drawings of strange plants, stars and bathing figures. Carbon dating places its parchment around 1404–1438. Despite efforts by cryptographers, linguists and computers, no one has convincingly read it."],["Everyday encryption","When a web address begins with ‘https’ and shows a padlock, the connection is encrypted. Messaging apps with end-to-end encryption mean that only the sender and receiver can read messages, not even the company running the service."]],
sp:["Make your own cipher","A pigpen cipher replaces letters with symbols drawn from grids, and was used by Freemasons in the 18th century. A Vigenère cipher uses a keyword to apply different shifts to each letter and was once called ‘the indecipherable cipher’ until it was broken in the 19th century."],
g:[["Cipher","A method of changing letters to hide a message"],["Key","Secret information used to encrypt or decrypt"],["Encryption","Turning information into a secret form"],["Cryptanalysis","Breaking codes and ciphers"]],
f:["The Caesar cipher shifts each letter along the alphabet.","Navajo code talkers’ code was never broken.","Online banking relies on the difficulty of factoring large numbers."],
fun:"During World War II, Bletchley Park recruited some code-breakers through a newspaper crossword competition; people who solved it quickly were invited for interviews."});

ADDART({id:"corrosion",t:"Rust and Corrosion",c:"Science",i:"🔩",w:"Corrosion",r:["metals","reactions","oxygen","steel","statueofliberty"],
p:[["Metals wearing away","Corrosion is the slow destruction of materials, especially metals, by chemical reactions."],["Rust","Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen and water."],["Costly","Corrosion costs the world trillions of dollars a year."]],
l:"Corrosion is the gradual destruction of materials, usually metals, by chemical reactions with their environment. The most familiar example is rust, the flaky reddish-brown iron oxide that forms when iron or steel reacts with oxygen and water. Salt speeds up rusting, which is why cars, ships and bridges near the sea or on salted winter roads corrode faster. Other metals corrode too: copper develops a green layer called patina, as seen on the Statue of Liberty, and silver tarnishes black. Unlike rust, these layers can protect the metal underneath. Corrosion weakens structures and machines and can cause dangerous failures, and studies estimate it costs about 3 to 4 percent of the world’s economic output each year. People fight corrosion with paint and coatings, galvanizing (coating steel with zinc), stainless steel, which contains chromium, and cathodic protection, in which a more reactive metal corrodes instead of the structure being protected.",
s:[["The chemistry of rust","Rusting is an electrochemical reaction. On the surface of iron, some areas lose electrons and iron dissolves, while elsewhere oxygen and water gain electrons. The iron combines with oxygen and water to form hydrated iron oxide. Because rust is porous and flakes off, fresh metal is exposed and rusting continues."],["Protective layers","Aluminum, chromium and titanium form thin, tough oxide layers that stop further corrosion, which is why aluminum cans do not rust away. The Statue of Liberty’s copper skin turned from shiny brown to green between the 1880s and about 1920 as patina formed, and the green layer now protects it."],["Fighting corrosion","Paint, oil and plastic coatings keep water and oxygen away. Galvanized steel is coated with zinc, which corrodes first. Stainless steel, invented by Harry Brearley in Sheffield in 1913, contains chromium that forms a protective film. Ships and pipelines are protected with blocks of zinc or magnesium, called sacrificial anodes, that corrode in their place."],["When corrosion causes disasters","Corrosion has contributed to collapsed bridges, leaking pipelines and aircraft accidents. In 1988, part of the roof of an Aloha Airlines plane tore off in flight, partly because of corrosion and metal fatigue. Engineers inspect structures regularly and use sensors to detect hidden corrosion."]],
k:[["Most common example","Rust (hydrated iron oxide)"],["Needed for rust","Iron, oxygen and water"],["Copper’s corrosion layer","Patina (green)"],["Stainless steel invented","1913, Harry Brearley"],["Global cost","About 3–4% of world economic output"]],
d:[["c. 400","Iron pillar of Delhi made"],["1742","Galvanizing process described in France"],["1824","Humphry Davy proposes cathodic protection for ships"],["1913","Stainless steel invented"],["1920","Statue of Liberty’s green patina complete"],["2016","Study estimates corrosion costs 2.5 trillion dollars a year"]],
s2:[["The Iron Pillar of Delhi","An iron pillar more than 7 meters tall, made around 1,600 years ago, stands in Delhi with remarkably little rust. Scientists found that its iron contains phosphorus, which helped form a thin protective layer, combined with Delhi’s dry climate."],["Rust in nature","Iron oxides give Mars its red color, color red soils and rocks on Earth, and were used as some of the first paints by prehistoric artists. Banded iron formations, rocks made of layers of iron oxide, record when oxygen first built up in Earth’s atmosphere."]],
sp:["Weathering steel","Some steels are designed to rust on purpose. Weathering steel forms a stable rust layer that protects it, giving a rich brown color. It is used in sculptures such as the Angel of the North in England and in bridges that never need painting."],
g:[["Corrosion","The slow destruction of a material by chemical reaction"],["Oxide","A compound of an element with oxygen"],["Patina","A thin layer formed on metal surfaces over time"],["Galvanizing","Coating steel with zinc to protect it"]],
f:["Rust needs both oxygen and water.","The Statue of Liberty is green because of copper corrosion.","Stainless steel resists rust because it contains chromium."],
fun:"The planet Mars is red because its surface dust is full of iron oxide, so Mars is, in a sense, a rusty planet."});

ADDART({id:"plasma",t:"Plasma, the Fourth State of Matter",c:"Science",i:"⚡",w:"Plasma (physics)",r:["states","sun","stars","fusion","aurora"],
p:[["Superheated gas","Plasma is a hot gas in which atoms have lost electrons."],["Most common state","Most of the visible matter in the universe, including stars, is plasma."],["Everyday plasma","Lightning, neon signs and auroras are all plasma."]],
l:"Plasma is often called the fourth state of matter, alongside solids, liquids and gases. It forms when a gas is heated to very high temperatures or exposed to strong electric fields, so that electrons are stripped from atoms, leaving a mixture of free electrons and positively charged ions. Because it contains charged particles, plasma conducts electricity and responds strongly to magnetic fields, and it often glows. Although plasma is rare in everyday life on Earth, it makes up more than 99 percent of the visible matter in the universe, because stars, including the Sun, are giant balls of plasma. On Earth, lightning, auroras, flames at very high temperature, neon and fluorescent lights and welding arcs contain plasma. Scientists use plasma to make computer chips, sterilize medical equipment and cut metal, and researchers are trying to harness the plasma inside fusion reactors to generate clean energy.",
s:[["Discovery","In the 1870s, William Crookes studied glowing gases in tubes and called this ‘radiant matter’. In 1928, the American chemist Irving Langmuir named it plasma, comparing the way it carries electrons and ions to blood plasma carrying blood cells."],["Plasma in space","The Sun’s surface is about 5,500 °C, and its outer atmosphere, the corona, is over a million degrees. The solar wind is a stream of plasma flowing outward from the Sun. When it reaches Earth, its particles follow the magnetic field toward the poles and make atoms in the upper atmosphere glow as auroras."],["Plasma on Earth","A lightning bolt heats air to around 30,000 °C, briefly turning it into plasma. In neon signs and fluorescent lamps, electricity turns gas into a glowing plasma. Plasma globes, popular toys, show glowing streams of plasma that follow your fingers when you touch the glass."],["Using plasma","Factories use plasma to etch tiny circuits on computer chips and to coat materials. Plasma torches cut through thick steel. Plasma televisions, popular from the late 1990s until the early 2010s, used millions of tiny cells of glowing gas. In fusion research, machines such as tokamaks hold plasma hotter than the Sun’s core with powerful magnetic fields."]],
k:[["Made of","Free electrons and ions"],["Named by","Irving Langmuir, 1928"],["Share of visible universe","Over 99%"],["Natural examples","Stars, lightning, auroras"],["Human uses","Lights, chip-making, cutting, fusion research"]],
d:[["1879","Crookes describes ‘radiant matter’"],["1928","Langmuir names plasma"],["1958","First tokamak experiments in the Soviet Union"],["1964","First plasma display panel invented"],["2014","Last major plasma TV production ends"],["2022","Fusion experiment produces more energy than the laser input"]],
s2:[["Plasma and fusion","In fusion, light atoms such as hydrogen join together, releasing huge energy, as in the Sun. To make fusion happen on Earth, plasma must be heated to over 100 million degrees and held in place without touching the walls. The international ITER project in France is building one of the largest fusion machines."],["Plasma in medicine","‘Cold plasma’ at room temperature can kill bacteria and viruses without heat, and is being studied for sterilizing equipment, healing wounds and keeping food fresh."]],
sp:["Ball lightning","Ball lightning is a rare phenomenon in which glowing spheres appear during thunderstorms, floating for a few seconds. It may involve plasma, but scientists are still not sure what causes it, and it is very hard to study because it is unpredictable."],
g:[["Plasma","A gas of charged particles: ions and free electrons"],["Ion","An atom with an electric charge"],["Electron","A tiny negatively charged particle in atoms"],["Tokamak","A doughnut-shaped machine for holding fusion plasma"]],
f:["Stars are made of plasma.","Lightning briefly heats air to about 30,000 °C.","Plasma was named after blood plasma."],
fun:"A lightning bolt is about five times hotter than the surface of the Sun."});

ADDART({id:"spacefood",t:"Space Food",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🍽️",w:"Space food",r:["astronauts","iss","nutri","marsrovers","artemis"],
p:[["Eating in orbit","Astronauts need food that is safe, light, nutritious and easy to eat in weightlessness."],["From tubes to tortillas","Early space food came in tubes; today astronauts eat a wide variety of meals."],["Growing food in space","Astronauts have grown and eaten lettuce and other plants on the ISS."]],
l:"Space food is food specially prepared for astronauts to eat in space. It must be safe, nutritious, light, compact, long-lasting and easy to eat in weightlessness, where crumbs and liquids float and can damage equipment or be inhaled. The first person to eat in space was Soviet cosmonaut Gherman Titov in 1961, and American John Glenn ate applesauce from a tube in 1962, proving that people could swallow in weightlessness. Early meals were puréed food in tubes and bite-sized cubes, but today astronauts on the International Space Station choose from more than 100 items, including freeze-dried and thermostabilized meals, fresh fruit delivered by supply ships and foods from different countries. Because taste is dulled in space, many astronauts prefer spicy foods. For future long missions to the Moon and Mars, scientists are developing ways to grow crops and produce food in space.",
s:[["Early space food","Titov ate a paste from a tube and reported feeling hungry. Glenn’s applesauce test in 1962 showed digestion worked. On Gemini 3 in 1965, astronaut John Young secretly brought a corned beef sandwich aboard, which shed crumbs and earned a reprimand. Apollo astronauts ate rehydratable food using hot water."],["Preparing food","Freeze-drying removes water so food stays light and keeps for years; astronauts add water before eating. Thermostabilized foods are heated to kill germs and sealed in pouches. Tortillas replaced bread on many missions because they make fewer crumbs. Salt and pepper come as liquids, because grains would float away."],["Eating on the ISS","Astronauts eat with spoons from pouches, using magnets, Velcro and tape to hold things down. Crews share meals from their countries, from Japanese ramen to Russian borscht. Fresh fruit is a treat after a supply ship arrives. Astronauts have a weekly menu and drink recycled water."],["Farming in space","In 2015, astronauts on the ISS ate red romaine lettuce grown in the station’s Veggie plant chamber, the first food grown and eaten by Americans in space. Plants such as radishes, chili peppers and cabbage have since been grown. Future Moon and Mars bases may use greenhouses, algae and even insects as food."]],
k:[["First to eat in space","Gherman Titov, 1961"],["First American to eat in space","John Glenn, 1962"],["ISS menu","More than 100 items"],["Popular bread substitute","Tortillas"],["First US space-grown food eaten","Lettuce, 2015"]],
d:[["1961","Titov eats in space"],["1962","John Glenn eats applesauce in orbit"],["1965","Smuggled corned beef sandwich on Gemini 3"],["1973","Skylab has a freezer and a dining table"],["1985","Tortillas first used on a shuttle mission"],["2015","Astronauts eat lettuce grown on the ISS"]],
s2:[["Why food tastes different","In weightlessness, body fluids shift toward the head, causing a blocked nose like a cold. Because smell is important for flavor, food tastes blander, so astronauts often add hot sauce and spices."],["Food for Mars","A trip to Mars and back could take two to three years, longer than most packaged food stays fresh and nutritious. NASA and other agencies have run competitions to develop new ways of producing food on long missions."]],
sp:["The space sandwich","When John Young pulled a corned beef sandwich from his pocket during Gemini 3 in 1965 and offered it to Gus Grissom, crumbs floated around the cabin. Congress held hearings about the incident, and the sandwich became a famous part of space history."],
g:[["Freeze-drying","Removing water by freezing and low pressure"],["Thermostabilized","Heated to kill germs and sealed"],["Weightlessness","Floating because of free fall in orbit"],["Rehydrate","To add water back to dried food"]],
f:["Salt and pepper are liquid on the ISS.","Tortillas are popular in space because they make few crumbs.","Food tastes blander in space."],
fun:"Astronauts have celebrated holidays on the ISS with special meals, including thermostabilized turkey and candied yams for American Thanksgiving."});

ADDART({id:"womeninspace",t:"Women in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Women in space",r:["astronauts","iss","artemis","spaceshuttle","feminism"],
p:[["Breaking barriers","Women have flown in space since Valentina Tereshkova in 1963."],["Records and firsts","Women have commanded spacecraft, walked in space and set endurance records."],["Around the Moon","In 2026, Christina Koch became the first woman to travel around the Moon."]],
l:"Women have played important roles in space exploration since the beginning of the space age, as astronauts, scientists, engineers and mathematicians. The Soviet cosmonaut Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space on 16 June 1963, orbiting Earth 48 times. Nearly twenty years passed before the next woman, Svetlana Savitskaya, flew in 1982; in 1984 she became the first woman to walk in space. Sally Ride became the first American woman in space in 1983, and Mae Jemison the first African American woman in 1992. Women have since commanded space shuttles and the International Space Station, conducted spacewalks and set records: Peggy Whitson has spent more time in space than any other American, and Christina Koch made the longest single spaceflight by a woman. In 2019, Koch and Jessica Meir carried out the first all-woman spacewalk, and in April 2026 Koch flew around the Moon on Artemis II, the first woman to do so. Women have flown for many countries, including the Soviet Union and Russia, the United States, China, Japan, Canada, France, the United Kingdom and South Korea.",
s:[["Early pioneers","In 1960–61, a privately funded program tested 13 American women pilots, later called the ‘Mercury 13’, who passed many of the same tests as male astronauts, but NASA did not accept women then. Tereshkova, a textile worker and amateur parachutist, was chosen from a group of female cosmonaut candidates."],["Shuttle era","Sally Ride flew on the shuttle Challenger in 1983 and later served on the panels investigating the Challenger and Columbia disasters. Kathryn Sullivan became the first American woman to walk in space in 1984. Eileen Collins became the first woman to pilot the shuttle in 1995 and the first to command it in 1999. Kalpana Chawla, the first India-born woman in space, died in the Columbia disaster in 2003."],["Space stations","Peggy Whitson became the first woman to command the ISS in 2007 and later set the American record for total time in space. Liu Yang became China’s first woman in space in 2012, and Wang Yaping made China’s first spacewalk by a woman in 2021. Christina Koch spent 328 days in orbit in 2019–20."],["Artemis","NASA’s Artemis program aims to land the first woman on the Moon. On Artemis II, launched on 1 April 2026, Christina Koch and three crewmates flew around the Moon and returned safely about ten days later, the first humans to travel to the Moon’s vicinity since 1972."]],
k:[["First woman in space","Valentina Tereshkova, 16 June 1963"],["First woman to walk in space","Svetlana Savitskaya, 1984"],["First American woman in space","Sally Ride, 1983"],["First all-woman spacewalk","18 October 2019"],["First woman around the Moon","Christina Koch, Artemis II, April 2026"]],
d:[["1963","Tereshkova becomes the first woman in space"],["1983","Sally Ride flies"],["1984","Savitskaya’s spacewalk; Sullivan’s spacewalk"],["1992","Mae Jemison flies"],["1999","Eileen Collins commands a shuttle"],["2019","First all-woman spacewalk"],["2026","Christina Koch flies around the Moon"]],
s2:[["Hidden figures","Women mathematicians such as Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan and Mary Jackson calculated trajectories for NASA’s early missions, work celebrated in the 2016 book and film Hidden Figures. Margaret Hamilton led the team that wrote the software for the Apollo guidance computer."],["Designing for everyone","In 2019, a planned all-woman spacewalk was postponed because not enough medium-sized spacesuit parts were ready on the station. The incident highlighted how equipment designed mainly for men can limit opportunities, and newer suits are designed to fit a wider range of body sizes."]],
sp:["Tereshkova’s call sign","Valentina Tereshkova’s call sign was Chaika, Russian for ‘seagull’. During her flight she kept a diary and took photographs used to study the atmosphere. She later became a politician and remains the only woman to have flown solo in space."],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Spacewalk","Activity outside a spacecraft in space"],["Commander","The person in charge of a mission"],["Call sign","A code name used in radio communication"]],
f:["Tereshkova is the only woman to fly a solo space mission.","The first all-woman spacewalk was in 2019.","Christina Koch was the first woman to travel around the Moon."],
fun:"Sally Ride was also an author, writing science books for children, and founded a company to inspire girls to study science."});

ADDART({id:"edgeofspace",t:"Where Space Begins",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Kármán line",r:["atmosphere","rocket","commspace","sat","astronauts"],
p:[["No sharp edge","Earth’s atmosphere fades gradually into space."],["The Kármán line","Space is commonly said to begin 100 kilometers above sea level."],["Closer than you think","Space is only about as far away as a short car journey, straight up."]],
l:"There is no sharp boundary where Earth’s atmosphere ends and outer space begins; the air simply gets thinner with height until it fades into the near-vacuum of space. For practical purposes, however, a boundary is needed for records, laws and astronaut awards. The most widely used boundary is the Kármán line, 100 kilometers above sea level, named after the Hungarian-American engineer Theodore von Kármán, who calculated roughly where the air becomes too thin for aircraft wings to provide lift. The World Air Sports Federation (FAI) uses 100 kilometers, while the United States Air Force and NASA award astronaut wings to people who fly above 50 miles (about 80 kilometers). Commercial spaceflights have made the definition matter, because some suborbital tourist flights go above 100 kilometers and others above 80. For comparison, the International Space Station orbits at about 400 kilometers, and satellites can orbit much higher.",
s:[["Layers of the atmosphere","About three-quarters of the air lies in the lowest layer, the troposphere, up to about 12 kilometers, where weather happens and planes fly. Above are the stratosphere, with the ozone layer, the mesosphere, where meteors burn up, and the thermosphere, where auroras glow and the ISS orbits. The outermost exosphere fades into space thousands of kilometers up."],["The Kármán line","Von Kármán calculated that at around 80–100 kilometers, an aircraft would need to fly so fast to stay up that it would effectively be in orbit. The 100-kilometer figure became popular because it is a round number. Some scientists have argued that 80 kilometers is closer to the true physical boundary."],["Record jumps","In 1960, US Air Force officer Joseph Kittinger jumped from a balloon at about 31 kilometers. In 2012, Felix Baumgartner jumped from about 39 kilometers and broke the sound barrier in free fall. In 2014, Alan Eustace jumped from about 41 kilometers. None of these reached space, but they showed how thin the air is so high up."],["Space tourism","Blue Origin’s New Shepard capsule flies above 100 kilometers, giving passengers a few minutes of weightlessness and a view of Earth’s curve against black sky. Virgin Galactic’s spaceplane flew to over 80 kilometers. Orbital flights, which circle Earth, require much more speed, about 28,000 kilometers per hour."]],
k:[["Kármán line","100 km above sea level"],["US astronaut boundary","50 miles (about 80 km)"],["Named after","Theodore von Kármán"],["ISS altitude","About 400 km"],["Highest skydive","About 41 km (2014)"]],
d:[["1946","A V-2 rocket photographs Earth from about 105 km"],["1957","Sputnik 1 reaches orbit"],["1960","Kittinger’s balloon jump from 31 km"],["2004","SpaceShipOne makes the first private crewed spaceflight"],["2012","Baumgartner’s jump from 39 km"],["2021","Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic fly tourists"]],
s2:[["How thin is the air?","At the top of Mount Everest, about 8.8 kilometers up, the air is about a third as dense as at sea level. At 100 kilometers, it is about a millionth of sea-level density. Even at the height of the ISS, there is just enough air to slowly drag the station down, so it must regularly boost itself higher."],["Why define space?","International law says that countries control the airspace above them, but outer space is free for all to explore. Without an agreed line, it is unclear where national airspace ends, which matters for satellites, spaceplanes and future traffic rules."]],
sp:["‘An hour’s drive away’","The astronomer Fred Hoyle once remarked that space isn’t remote at all, but only an hour’s drive away if your car could go straight upwards. At highway speeds of 100 kilometers per hour, you would reach the Kármán line in about an hour."],
g:[["Atmosphere","The layer of gases surrounding a planet"],["Suborbital","Reaching space but not circling the Earth"],["Orbit","A curved path around a planet or star"],["Vacuum","A space with almost no matter"]],
f:["Space is commonly said to begin 100 kilometers up.","The US counts people flying above 80 kilometers as astronauts.","Driving straight up at highway speed, you would reach space in about an hour."],
fun:"The first photo of Earth from space was taken on 24 October 1946 by a camera on a captured German V-2 rocket launched from New Mexico."});

ADDART({id:"gems",t:"Gemstones",c:"Geography",i:"💎",w:"Gemstone",r:["rocks","crystals","carbonchem","metals","jewelry"],
p:[["Precious stones","Gemstones are minerals cut and polished for jewelry and decoration."],["Rare and beautiful","Diamonds, rubies, sapphires and emeralds are the best known."],["Formed deep underground","Most gems form under heat and pressure over millions of years."]],
l:"A gemstone is a mineral, rock or organic material that is cut and polished for use in jewelry or decoration because of its beauty, durability and rarity. Most gems are crystals that formed deep in the Earth under high heat and pressure or from mineral-rich fluids over millions of years. Traditionally, diamond, ruby, sapphire and emerald have been called precious stones, while others such as amethyst, topaz, garnet and opal are called semi-precious, although the distinction is not scientific. Some gems come from living things: pearls form in oysters and mussels, amber is fossilized tree resin and coral is made by tiny sea animals. Gems are valued by the ‘four Cs’: color, clarity, cut and carat weight. Diamond, the hardest natural material, is pure carbon. Since the 20th century, scientists have learned to grow synthetic gems, and laboratory-made diamonds are now common. Concerns about ‘blood diamonds’ and mining conditions have led to efforts to trace where gems come from.",
s:[["How gems form","Diamonds form about 150–200 kilometers deep in the Earth’s mantle and are carried up by volcanic eruptions through pipes of rock called kimberlite. Emeralds and many other gems crystallize from hot fluids in cracks in rocks. Some, such as opal, form from silica-rich water seeping into rocks near the surface."],["Famous gems","Ruby and sapphire are both forms of the mineral corundum; red corundum is ruby, and other colors are sapphire. Emerald is green beryl, colored by chromium. The Cullinan diamond, found in South Africa in 1905, weighed 3,106 carats and was cut into several stones in the British Crown Jewels."],["Cutting and value","Gem cutters study each stone to decide how to cut it, creating facets that reflect light and show off color and sparkle. A carat is a unit of weight equal to 0.2 grams. Color, clarity (how free of flaws a stone is), cut and carat weight together determine value."],["Ethics and synthetic gems","‘Blood diamonds’ mined in war zones funded conflicts in countries such as Sierra Leone and Angola in the 1990s, leading to the Kimberley Process certification scheme in 2003. Laboratory-grown diamonds, first made in 1954, are chemically identical to mined diamonds and have become much cheaper and more popular."]],
k:[["Traditional precious stones","Diamond, ruby, sapphire, emerald"],["Hardest natural material","Diamond (10 on the Mohs scale)"],["One carat","0.2 grams"],["Largest gem diamond found","Cullinan, 3,106 carats (1905)"],["Organic gems","Pearl, amber, coral"]],
d:[["1812","Friedrich Mohs creates his hardness scale"],["1867","Diamonds discovered in South Africa"],["1905","Cullinan diamond found"],["1954","First laboratory-grown diamonds made"],["2003","Kimberley Process begins"]],
s2:[["The Mohs scale","In 1812 the German mineralogist Friedrich Mohs ranked minerals by which could scratch which, from talc (1) to diamond (10). Quartz is 7, and gems harder than that resist scratching from everyday dust, which contains quartz."],["Birthstones","Many cultures link gems to months of birth, such as garnet for January and ruby for July. The modern Western list was set by American jewelers in 1912, but the idea goes back to ancient times."]],
sp:["The Hope Diamond","The Hope Diamond, a deep blue diamond of 45.52 carats, has a long history passing through French kings and wealthy owners, along with legends of a curse. It was donated to the Smithsonian Institution in 1958 and is one of the most visited museum objects in the world."],
g:[["Gemstone","A mineral cut and polished for jewelry"],["Carat","A unit of weight for gems, 0.2 grams"],["Facet","A flat, polished surface on a cut gem"],["Synthetic","Made in a laboratory rather than found in nature"]],
f:["Ruby and sapphire are the same mineral, corundum.","Diamond is pure carbon.","Pearls and amber come from living things."],
fun:"The Hope Diamond was mailed to the Smithsonian in 1958 in a plain brown package by registered mail."});

ADDART({id:"jetstream",t:"The Jet Stream",c:"Geography",i:"💨",w:"Jet stream",r:["atmosphere","weatherforecast","flight","storms","climchange"],
p:[["Rivers of wind","Jet streams are fast, narrow bands of wind high in the atmosphere."],["Shaping weather","They steer storms and separate cold and warm air."],["Helping planes","Planes flying east can ride the jet stream to save time and fuel."]],
l:"A jet stream is a fast-flowing, narrow current of air high in the atmosphere, usually 9 to 16 kilometers above the ground, near the top of the troposphere. The strongest jet streams flow from west to east and can reach speeds of more than 300 kilometers per hour. They form where cold polar air meets warmer air from the tropics, creating sharp differences in temperature and pressure, combined with the Earth’s rotation. Each hemisphere has a polar jet stream and a weaker subtropical jet stream. Jet streams meander in large waves around the planet, steering weather systems: storms often follow the polar jet, and a wavy or stuck jet stream can bring long periods of heat, cold or rain. Pilots use jet streams to speed up flights heading east and avoid them going west. Scientists are studying how climate change, by warming the Arctic faster than other regions, may be affecting the jet stream’s shape.",
s:[["Discovery","In the 1920s, the Japanese meteorologist Wasaburo Oishi measured strong high-altitude winds over Japan using weather balloons, publishing his results in Esperanto, so they were little known. During World War II, American bomber pilots flying to Japan encountered extremely strong headwinds, and the term ‘jet stream’ became widely used."],["How it forms","The difference in temperature between the cold poles and the warm tropics creates differences in air pressure, driving winds. The Earth’s rotation deflects these winds to flow east, a result of the Coriolis effect. The jet stream is strongest in winter, when the temperature contrast is greatest."],["Weather and the jet stream","When the jet stream flows straight, weather changes quickly. When it develops big loops, it can let cold Arctic air plunge south or warm air push north, and when loops stall, they can cause heat waves, droughts or floods that last for weeks. Forecasters watch the jet stream closely."],["Flying with the jet stream","Flights from North America to Europe are often shorter than the return journey because of tailwinds. In February 2020, a British Airways flight from New York to London, pushed by a strong jet stream during Storm Ciara, set a subsonic record of under five hours."]],
k:[["Height","About 9–16 km"],["Speed","Often over 300 km/h"],["Direction","Mainly west to east"],["Types","Polar and subtropical jets"],["Discovered","1920s, Wasaburo Oishi"]],
d:[["1920s","Wasaburo Oishi measures high winds over Japan"],["1944","US bomber pilots encounter the jet stream"],["1944–45","Japan launches balloon bombs carried by the jet stream"],["1950s","Airlines begin planning routes around jet streams"],["2020","Jet stream helps set a New York–London flight record"]],
s2:[["Balloon bombs","In 1944–45, Japan launched about 9,300 paper balloons carrying bombs into the jet stream, aiming to reach North America. Several hundred arrived, and one killed six people on a picnic in Oregon in May 1945, the only deaths from enemy action on the US mainland during World War II."],["Climate change","Some scientists suggest that because the Arctic is warming faster than the rest of the world, the temperature difference driving the jet stream is shrinking, which may make it wavier and more likely to stall. Others think the evidence is not yet clear, and research continues."]],
sp:["Jet streams on other planets","Jupiter and Saturn have powerful jet streams that form their colorful bands, with winds over 500 kilometers per hour. Saturn’s north pole has a strange six-sided jet stream called the hexagon, larger than the Earth."],
g:[["Jet stream","A fast, narrow band of high-altitude wind"],["Troposphere","The lowest layer of the atmosphere, where weather happens"],["Coriolis effect","The bending of moving air caused by Earth’s rotation"],["Tailwind","Wind blowing in the direction of travel"]],
f:["Jet streams flow mostly from west to east.","They can reach speeds of more than 300 km/h.","Flights going east are often faster because of the jet stream."],
fun:"Wasaburo Oishi published his discovery of the jet stream in Esperanto, an invented international language, which is one reason his work went unnoticed for years."});

ADDART({id:"oceancurrents",t:"Ocean Currents",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Ocean current",r:["pacific","atlantic","climate","elnino","plasticwaste","oceanacidification","seamounts"],
p:[["Rivers in the sea","Ocean currents are continuous movements of seawater."],["Moving heat","Currents carry warm and cold water around the globe, shaping climate."],["The Gulf Stream","The Gulf Stream helps keep northwestern Europe mild."]],
l:"Ocean currents are continuous, directed movements of seawater, flowing like huge rivers within the oceans. Surface currents are driven mainly by winds and shaped by the Earth’s rotation and the shapes of continents, forming giant circular systems called gyres in each major ocean basin. Deeper currents are driven by differences in water temperature and saltiness, which change its density; cold, salty water sinks near the poles and slowly spreads through the deep oceans, in a worldwide circulation sometimes called the global conveyor belt. Currents carry heat from the tropics toward the poles, influencing climate: the Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Current help make northwestern Europe much milder than places at similar latitudes in Canada. Currents also carry nutrients that support fisheries, transport plankton, larvae and floating debris, and have guided sailors and explorers for centuries. Scientists are monitoring signs that the Atlantic circulation may be weakening as the climate warms.",
s:[["Surface currents","Steady trade winds and westerlies push surface water, and the Coriolis effect turns currents clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and anticlockwise in the Southern Hemisphere. The Antarctic Circumpolar Current, flowing around Antarctica, is the largest current, carrying more than 100 times the flow of all the world’s rivers."],["Deep currents","In the North Atlantic and around Antarctica, very cold, salty water sinks and flows along the ocean floor. It may take about 1,000 years for water to complete the full journey of the global conveyor belt. This circulation helps store heat and carbon dioxide in the deep ocean."],["Currents and climate","The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), which includes the Gulf Stream, moves vast amounts of heat northward. Studies suggest it has weakened and could slow further as melting ice adds fresh water. The Humboldt Current off South America brings cold, nutrient-rich water that supports huge fish populations, and changes during El Niño can disrupt it."],["Tracking currents","Benjamin Franklin published one of the first charts of the Gulf Stream in 1770 to help mail ships cross the Atlantic faster. Today, satellites measure sea surface height and temperature, and thousands of floating robots called Argo floats dive and surface to record ocean conditions."]],
k:[["Driven by","Wind, Earth’s rotation, temperature and saltiness"],["Largest current","Antarctic Circumpolar Current"],["Famous warm current","Gulf Stream"],["Global conveyor belt cycle","About 1,000 years"],["First Gulf Stream chart","Benjamin Franklin, 1770"]],
d:[["1513","Ponce de León’s ships encounter the Gulf Stream"],["1770","Franklin publishes a Gulf Stream chart"],["1872–76","HMS Challenger expedition studies the oceans"],["1992","Thousands of bath toys spill into the Pacific"],["2000","Argo float program begins"],["2010s","Studies report signs of AMOC weakening"]],
s2:[["Garbage patches","Ocean gyres collect floating plastic in areas such as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, between Hawaii and California. Most of the plastic is in small pieces, making it hard to clean up, and it harms sea life."],["Currents and migration","Sea turtles, eels and whales use currents to travel vast distances. Baby loggerhead turtles from Florida ride the Gulf Stream into the North Atlantic gyre, drifting for years before returning to coastal waters."]],
sp:["The rubber ducks","In January 1992, a container of 28,800 plastic bath toys, including yellow ducks, fell off a ship in the North Pacific. Over the following years, the ‘Friendly Floatees’ washed up in Alaska, Hawaii and even the Atlantic, helping oceanographers study currents."],
g:[["Ocean current","A continuous flow of seawater"],["Gyre","A large circular system of ocean currents"],["Density","How heavy something is for its size"],["Upwelling","Cold, deep water rising to the surface"]],
f:["The Antarctic Circumpolar Current is the world’s largest current.","Deep ocean water may take about 1,000 years to circulate.","The Gulf Stream helps keep Britain and Ireland mild."],
fun:"Benjamin Franklin noticed that British mail ships took weeks longer to cross the Atlantic than American whaling ships, because the whalers knew to avoid sailing against the Gulf Stream."});

ADDART({id:"clouds",t:"Clouds",c:"Geography",i:"☁️",w:"Cloud",r:["wcycle","storms","weatherforecast","thunderstorms","rainbows","fog","hail"],
p:[["Floating water","Clouds are made of tiny water droplets or ice crystals."],["Named in Latin","Clouds are classified by shape and height, using names like cumulus and cirrus."],["Weather clues","Clouds help forecast weather and affect Earth’s climate."]],
l:"A cloud is a visible mass of tiny water droplets or ice crystals floating in the atmosphere. Clouds form when air containing water vapor rises and cools, so the vapor condenses onto tiny particles of dust, salt or smoke. Although clouds look light, a typical fluffy cumulus cloud contains hundreds of metric tons of water, spread out in droplets so small they float. In 1802, the English chemist Luke Howard proposed naming clouds with Latin words describing their shape: cumulus (‘heap’), stratus (‘layer’), cirrus (‘curl of hair’) and nimbus (‘rain cloud’), a system still used today. The World Meteorological Organization’s International Cloud Atlas recognizes ten main types, grouped by height. Clouds bring rain and snow, warn of storms and shape climate, both reflecting sunlight back into space and trapping heat. Watching clouds has long been a way for farmers, sailors and pilots to predict the weather.",
s:[["How clouds form","As moist air rises, for example when warmed by the ground, pushed up mountains or forced over colder air, it expands and cools. When it cools to its dew point, water vapor condenses into droplets. Without tiny particles called condensation nuclei, droplets would struggle to form."],["Types of clouds","High clouds, such as wispy cirrus, are made of ice crystals. Mid-level clouds include altocumulus and altostratus. Low clouds include stratus, which can form gray blankets or fog, and stratocumulus. Cumulus and cumulonimbus grow upward; a cumulonimbus thunderstorm cloud can tower 15 kilometers or more."],["Rain, snow and hail","Cloud droplets are far too small to fall. They must collide and merge, or ice crystals must grow at the expense of droplets, until they become heavy enough to fall as rain or snow. Strong updrafts in thunderstorms can carry ice up and down repeatedly, building hailstones."],["Clouds and climate","Low, thick clouds tend to cool the Earth by reflecting sunlight, while high, thin clouds tend to warm it by trapping heat. How clouds will change as the climate warms is one of the biggest uncertainties in climate science. Aircraft contrails, made of ice crystals from engine exhaust, add artificial clouds."]],
k:[["Made of","Water droplets or ice crystals"],["Naming system","Luke Howard, 1802"],["Main types","Ten, in the International Cloud Atlas"],["Tallest clouds","Cumulonimbus, up to 15+ km"],["Highest clouds","Noctilucent clouds, about 80 km"]],
d:[["1802","Luke Howard names the clouds"],["1896","First International Cloud Atlas published"],["1960","First weather satellite images of clouds"],["2005","Cloud Appreciation Society founded"],["2017","New cloud types, such as asperitas, added to the Atlas"]],
s2:[["Unusual clouds","Lenticular clouds, shaped like lenses or flying saucers, form over mountains. Mammatus clouds hang like pouches under storm clouds. Noctilucent clouds, the highest on Earth, glow blue in summer twilight near the poles. Asperitas, with wave-like undersides, was officially recognized in 2017 after cloud-watchers shared photos."],["Cloud seeding","Cloud seeding sprays tiny particles, such as silver iodide, into clouds to encourage rain or snow. It is used in countries including China, the United Arab Emirates and the United States, though scientists debate how much extra rain it produces."]],
sp:["How heavy is a cloud?","Scientists estimate that an average cumulus cloud, about a kilometer across, contains roughly 500 metric tons of water, about as heavy as 100 elephants. It floats because the water is spread out in billions of tiny droplets supported by rising air."],
g:[["Condensation","Water vapor turning into liquid"],["Dew point","The temperature at which air becomes saturated"],["Cumulus","A puffy, heaped cloud"],["Cirrus","A thin, wispy high cloud made of ice"]],
f:["An average cumulus cloud holds about 500 metric tons of water.","Cloud names come from Latin.","Fog is a cloud touching the ground."],
fun:"The phrase ‘on cloud nine’ may come from an early cloud classification in which cloud number nine was the towering cumulonimbus, the highest of all."});

ADDART({id:"recommender",t:"Recommendation Algorithms",c:"Technology",i:"🎯",w:"Recommender system",r:["algorithm","mlearn","socialmedia","streaming","dataprivacy"],
p:[["‘You might also like’","Recommendation algorithms suggest videos, products, songs and posts."],["Everywhere online","They power feeds on YouTube, TikTok, Netflix, Spotify and online shops."],["Helpful and controversial","They help people find things, but raise concerns about addiction and bubbles."]],
l:"A recommendation algorithm, or recommender system, is software that predicts what a person is likely to want and suggests it, such as the next video to watch, a product to buy, a song to play or a post to see. Recommender systems analyze huge amounts of data, including what you have clicked, watched, bought, liked and skipped, and compare it with the behavior of millions of other users. Early systems used collaborative filtering, the idea that people who agreed in the past will agree again, and content-based filtering, which suggests items similar to ones you liked. Modern systems use machine learning and deep neural networks. They are central to online platforms: Netflix has said most of what members watch comes from recommendations, and YouTube has said recommendations drive most of its watch time. They help people discover things they enjoy, but critics argue they can encourage endless scrolling, spread extreme or misleading content, create ‘filter bubbles’ and depend on extensive personal data.",
s:[["How they work","Collaborative filtering looks for patterns: if many people who liked film A also liked film B, someone who likes A may be shown B. Content-based systems use features of items, such as genre or keywords. Modern platforms combine many signals, including how long you watch, what time it is and what device you use, to rank thousands of possible items in a fraction of a second."],["History","In the early 1990s, research projects such as GroupLens recommended news articles and films based on ratings. Amazon introduced item-to-item recommendations (‘customers who bought this also bought’) in the late 1990s. From 2006 to 2009, Netflix offered a one-million-dollar prize to anyone who could improve its recommendations by 10 percent; a team of researchers won in 2009."],["The feed","Social media feeds, once shown in time order, are now mostly ranked by algorithms predicting what will keep users engaged. TikTok’s ‘For You’ page, which quickly learns users’ tastes from their viewing, made short video recommendations especially powerful."],["Concerns and rules","Critics worry that maximizing engagement can favor sensational content and make it hard to stop scrolling, especially for young people. The term ‘filter bubble’, coined by Eli Pariser in 2011, describes people seeing mainly views that match their own, though studies disagree on how strong this effect is. The EU’s Digital Services Act requires large platforms to explain how their recommendations work and offer a feed not based on profiling."]],
k:[["Main methods","Collaborative and content-based filtering"],["Netflix Prize","2006–2009, one million dollars"],["Famous phrase","‘Customers who bought this also bought’"],["‘Filter bubble’ coined","2011, Eli Pariser"],["EU rules","Digital Services Act"]],
d:[["1992","GroupLens and other early recommender research"],["1998","Amazon develops item-to-item recommendations"],["2006","Netflix Prize launched"],["2009","Netflix Prize won"],["2016","TikTok’s algorithm-driven feed launches in China as Douyin"],["2024","EU Digital Services Act fully applies"]],
s2:[["Taking control","Many apps let users tell the algorithm they are ‘not interested’, clear their watch history, mute topics or switch to a chronological feed. Choosing who to follow and searching deliberately also shape what you see."],["Recommendations offline","Recommendation systems are also used for matching job seekers with jobs, suggesting friends, planning delivery routes and helping doctors find relevant research. Libraries and bookshops have long done a human version of the same thing."]],
sp:["The long tail","Recommendation systems help people discover less popular items, such as niche books, films and songs, that physical shops could not stock. The writer Chris Anderson called this the ‘long tail’ in 2004, arguing that many small sales could add up to a big market."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of step-by-step instructions for a computer"],["Collaborative filtering","Recommending based on similar users’ choices"],["Engagement","How much people interact with content"],["Filter bubble","Seeing mostly information that matches your views"]],
f:["Most viewing on Netflix comes from recommendations.","Netflix once offered a one-million-dollar prize to improve its recommendations.","The EU requires large platforms to offer a feed not based on profiling."],
fun:"The team that won the Netflix Prize submitted its winning entry just 20 minutes before a rival team submitted one that scored exactly the same."});

ADDART({id:"coal",t:"Coal",c:"Technology",i:"⚫",w:"Coal",r:["fossilfuels","steam","airpollution","climchange","energytransition"],
p:[["Fossil fuel","Coal is a black rock formed from ancient plants, burned for energy."],["Fueled industry","Coal powered the Industrial Revolution and still makes over a third of the world’s electricity."],["High cost","Burning coal is the largest single source of carbon dioxide emissions."]],
l:"Coal is a black or brown sedimentary rock formed from the remains of plants that grew in swamps hundreds of millions of years ago, buried and compressed over time. It is mostly carbon and burns to release heat. Coal powered the Industrial Revolution, fueling steam engines, locomotives, ships, iron and steel works and gas lighting, and it transformed economies first in Britain and then around the world. Today coal is still used to generate more than a third of the world’s electricity and to make steel and cement. China burns more than half of the world’s coal, and India is the second-largest user. But coal is the most polluting fossil fuel: burning it releases more carbon dioxide per unit of energy than oil or gas, making it the largest single source of greenhouse gas emissions, and it causes air pollution that harms health. Coal mining has also been dangerous. Many countries plan to phase out coal power, and Britain closed its last coal power station in 2024.",
s:[["Formation","Most of the world’s coal formed during the Carboniferous period, about 359 to 299 million years ago, when vast swamp forests of giant ferns and tree-like plants covered parts of the land. As plants died, they formed peat, which was buried and pressed by layers of sediment, becoming lignite, then bituminous coal and finally hard anthracite."],["Coal and industry","Coal was used in China for heating and metalworking over 2,000 years ago. In 18th-century Britain, steam engines were first used to pump water from coal mines, and coal-fired engines then drove factories and railways. Coal towns grew around mines, and coal miners formed some of the first large trade unions."],["Mining dangers","Underground mining exposes workers to explosions of methane gas and coal dust, roof collapses and lung disease. The Courrières disaster in France in 1906 killed more than 1,000 miners, and the Benxihu colliery explosion in China in 1942 killed over 1,500. In 1966, a coal waste tip collapsed onto the village of Aberfan in Wales, killing 144 people, including 116 children."],["The future of coal","Many countries are replacing coal with gas, nuclear, wind and solar power. Britain, where industrial coal use began, closed its last coal power plant, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, on 30 September 2024. Yet global coal use reached record levels in the mid-2020s because of rising demand in Asia, and debates continue about how to help coal-mining communities in the transition."]],
k:[["Formed from","Ancient swamp plants"],["Main formation period","Carboniferous, 359–299 million years ago"],["Share of world electricity","More than a third"],["Largest user","China, over half of world consumption"],["Britain’s last coal plant closed","30 September 2024"]],
d:[["1712","Newcomen steam engine pumps water from a coal mine"],["1906","Courrières mining disaster in France"],["1942","Benxihu colliery disaster in China"],["1966","Aberfan disaster in Wales"],["2021","COP26 agrees to ‘phase down’ coal"],["2024","Britain closes its last coal power station"]],
s2:[["Types of coal","Peat is partly decayed plant matter, the first stage of coal formation. Lignite, or brown coal, is soft and crumbly. Bituminous coal is the most common, used in power stations and steel-making. Anthracite is hard, shiny and burns with little smoke but is the rarest."],["Coal and steel","Coking coal is heated without air to make coke, a nearly pure carbon fuel used in blast furnaces to turn iron ore into iron. Steelmakers are testing ways to replace coke with hydrogen to cut emissions."]],
sp:["Canaries in the mine","Until 1986, British coal miners took canaries underground because the birds were more sensitive to carbon monoxide gas than people. If a canary showed distress, miners knew to leave, giving rise to the phrase ‘canary in the coal mine’ for an early warning."],
g:[["Fossil fuel","A fuel formed from ancient living things, like coal, oil and gas"],["Carboniferous","The geological period when much coal formed"],["Anthracite","The hardest, highest-quality coal"],["Coke","A fuel made by heating coal without air"]],
f:["Most coal formed about 300 million years ago.","China burns more than half of the world’s coal.","Britain, birthplace of the Industrial Revolution, ended coal power in 2024."],
fun:"Jet, a black gemstone used in Victorian mourning jewelry, is a kind of coal formed from fossilized wood."});

ADDART({id:"locks",t:"Locks and Keys",c:"Technology",i:"🔑",w:"Lock and key",r:["machines","cybersecurity","ciphers","wheel","smarthome"],
p:[["Keeping things safe","Locks are devices that keep doors, boxes and containers closed until opened with a key or code."],["Ancient inventions","Wooden pin locks were used in Mesopotamia and Egypt about 4,000 years ago."],["From keys to codes","Modern locks use pin tumblers, combinations, cards, fingerprints and phones."]],
l:"A lock is a mechanical or electronic device that keeps a door, gate, container or vehicle closed until it is released by a key, code, card or other means. Some of the oldest known locks were large wooden pin locks used in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 4,000 years ago, opened with wooden keys that lifted pins out of a bolt. The Romans made metal locks and keys, some small enough to wear as rings. In the 18th and 19th centuries, inventors in Britain and America designed more secure locks, including Joseph Bramah’s famous challenge lock of 1784 and the pin tumbler lock developed by Linus Yale Jr. in the 1860s, still the most common door lock today. Combination locks, padlocks and safes followed. Modern locks include electronic keycards in hotels, keypads, fingerprint and face recognition and smart locks opened by phones. As locks improved, so did lock-picking, leading to a long contest between lock makers and those trying to defeat them.",
s:[["Ancient locks","The Egyptian-style pin lock had a wooden bolt with holes into which pins dropped from above, locking it. A large wooden key with matching pegs pushed the pins up so the bolt could slide. The ancient Greeks used simpler locks opened with hooked keys, and the Romans developed warded locks with internal obstacles that only the correct key could pass."],["The lock-picking race","In 1784, Joseph Bramah displayed a lock in his London shop window with a sign offering 200 guineas to anyone who could pick it. It remained unpicked for 67 years until the American locksmith Alfred Hobbs opened it in 1851 after about 51 hours of work, during the Great Exhibition, where he also picked other ‘unpickable’ locks."],["The pin tumbler lock","In a pin tumbler lock, spring-loaded pins of different lengths block a cylinder from turning. The right key pushes each pin to exactly the right height, lining them up so the cylinder turns. Linus Yale Sr. and his son Linus Yale Jr. developed modern versions in the mid-1800s, along with small flat keys."],["Electronic and smart locks","Hotel keycards, introduced from the 1970s, store codes on magnetic strips or chips. Smart locks connect to phones and the internet, letting owners unlock doors remotely or give temporary access. Security experts warn that electronic locks can be hacked, so they must be designed and updated carefully."]],
k:[["Oldest known locks","About 4,000 years ago, Mesopotamia and Egypt"],["Most common door lock","Pin tumbler lock"],["Developer of modern pin tumbler","Linus Yale Jr., 1860s"],["Bramah’s challenge","1784, picked in 1851"],["Modern types","Keycards, keypads, biometrics, smart locks"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BC","Wooden pin locks in Mesopotamia and Egypt"],["c. 100","Roman metal keys and ring keys"],["1778","Robert Barron patents the double-acting lever tumbler lock"],["1784","Bramah’s challenge lock"],["1851","Alfred Hobbs picks the Bramah lock"],["1861","Linus Yale Jr. patents a pin tumbler lock"],["1975","Electronic hotel keycards introduced"]],
s2:[["Combination locks","Combination locks open when dials are turned to the correct sequence of numbers. They are used on safes, bank vaults, luggage and bicycle locks. Bank vaults also use time locks that cannot be opened before a set time, even with the right combination."],["Lock sport","Lock sport is a hobby in which enthusiasts pick locks they own, or locks given to them with permission, to understand how they work. Security researchers use similar skills to find weaknesses that manufacturers can fix."]],
sp:["Houdini and locks","The escape artist Harry Houdini became famous in the early 1900s for escaping from handcuffs, jail cells and locked boxes, challenging police to restrain him. He studied locks carefully and sometimes hid small tools, keeping many of his methods secret."],
g:[["Lock","A device that keeps something closed"],["Tumbler","A movable part in a lock that must be set correctly to open it"],["Combination","A sequence of numbers that opens a lock"],["Biometric","Using body features, like fingerprints, for identification"]],
f:["Some of the oldest locks were made of wood.","Bramah’s lock went unpicked for 67 years.","Most door locks today are pin tumbler locks."],
fun:"Some Roman keys were made as rings so their owners could carry them on their fingers and keep them safe."});

ADDART({id:"uluru",t:"Uluru",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🪨",w:"Uluru",r:["australiacountry","aboriginal","desert","rocks","overtourism"],
p:[["A giant sandstone rock","Uluru is a huge sandstone rock in the desert of central Australia."],["Sacred to the Anangu","It is a sacred place for the Anangu, its Aboriginal owners."],["Changing colors","Uluru glows red and orange at sunrise and sunset."]],
l:"Uluru is an enormous sandstone rock formation, also known as Ayers Rock, in the desert of Australia’s Northern Territory, near the center of the continent. It rises about 348 meters above the surrounding plain, is about 3.6 kilometers long and has a circumference of about 9.4 kilometers, and much more of the rock lies hidden underground. Uluru is famous for appearing to change color, glowing deep red and orange at sunrise and sunset. It is sacred to the Anangu, the Aboriginal people of the area, whose ancestral stories, known in English as the Dreaming, explain its features, caves and waterholes, and who have lived there for tens of thousands of years. Together with the nearby domes of Kata Tjuta, it forms Uluṟu-Kata Tjuṯa National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Ownership was returned to the Anangu in 1985, and in 2019 climbing the rock was permanently banned out of respect for its cultural significance.",
s:[["Geology","Uluru is made of arkose, a coarse sandstone rich in feldspar, formed from sediments eroded from ancient mountains over 500 million years ago. Later earth movements tilted the layers nearly upright. Iron minerals in the rock rust when exposed to air, giving Uluru its red color; inside, the rock is gray."],["Anangu culture","For the Anangu, Uluru is a living cultural landscape. Tjukurpa, their system of law and knowledge, tells how ancestral beings shaped the land. Rock art in Uluru’s caves is thousands of years old. Some places around the base are especially sacred and should not be photographed."],["Handback","In 1873 the explorer William Gosse named the rock Ayers Rock after a South Australian politician. On 26 October 1985, the Australian government returned the title to the Anangu, who lease the land back to the national parks agency and manage the park jointly. The park was listed as a World Heritage Site in 1987 and recognized for its cultural values in 1994."],["Climbing ban","For decades, tourists climbed Uluru, although the Anangu asked them not to; at least 37 people died on the climb. The park board voted to close it, and the climb closed permanently on 26 October 2019. Visitors now walk around the base, take tours with Aboriginal guides and watch the sunset."]],
k:[["Height above plain","About 348 m"],["Length","About 3.6 km"],["Rock type","Arkose sandstone"],["Traditional owners","Anangu"],["Handback","26 October 1985"],["Climbing banned","26 October 2019"]],
d:[["Ancient times","Aboriginal people live in the area for tens of thousands of years"],["1873","William Gosse names it Ayers Rock"],["1985","Ownership returned to the Anangu"],["1987","World Heritage listing"],["2002","Official name becomes ‘Uluru / Ayers Rock’"],["2019","Climbing permanently banned"]],
s2:[["Kata Tjuta","About 25 kilometers west of Uluru, Kata Tjuta (‘many heads’) is a group of 36 rounded domes of conglomerate rock, the tallest rising higher than Uluru. It is also sacred to the Anangu, and the Valley of the Winds walk passes between the domes."],["Desert life","Despite the dry climate, the area supports red kangaroos, thorny devil lizards, many birds and hardy plants. After rare heavy rains, waterfalls pour down Uluru’s sides, a spectacular but brief sight."]],
sp:["The Field of Light","In 2016, British artist Bruce Munro installed the Field of Light near Uluru: tens of thousands of solar-powered glass spheres that glow in changing colors at night across the desert. It became one of the area’s most popular attractions."],
g:[["Sandstone","Rock formed from compressed sand"],["Anangu","The Aboriginal traditional owners of Uluru"],["Tjukurpa","Anangu law, knowledge and ancestral stories"],["World Heritage Site","A place protected by UNESCO for its importance"]],
f:["Uluru is 348 meters high.","Inside, the rock is gray; its red color comes from rusting iron.","Climbing Uluru was banned in 2019."],
fun:"Uluru’s color changes are so striking that many visitors photograph it every few minutes at sunset to capture the shift from orange to deep red to purple."});

ADDART({id:"tasmania",t:"Tasmania",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏝️",w:"Tasmania",r:["australiacountry","aboriginal","galapagos","trees","kangaroo","thylacine"],
p:[["Australia’s island state","Tasmania is a mountainous island state south of mainland Australia."],["Wild nature","Nearly half of Tasmania is protected parks and wilderness."],["Unique animals","It is the last home of the wild Tasmanian devil."]],
l:"Tasmania is an island state of Australia, lying about 240 kilometers south of the mainland across Bass Strait. With about 575,000 people, it is the smallest Australian state by area and population. Its capital is Hobart. Tasmania has rugged mountains, rainforests, rivers, lakes and beaches, and around 40 percent of its land is protected in national parks and reserves, including the Tasmanian Wilderness World Heritage Area. Aboriginal Tasmanians, the palawa, lived on the island for at least 40,000 years. The Dutch explorer Abel Tasman reached it in 1642, and the British established a colony in 1803, using it as a major destination for convicts. Colonization devastated the Aboriginal population through violence and disease. Tasmania is famous for unique wildlife, including the Tasmanian devil and, until its extinction in the 20th century, the thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger. Today its clean environment, food, history and arts attract many visitors.",
s:[["Aboriginal history","When rising seas flooded Bass Strait about 10,000–12,000 years ago, Aboriginal Tasmanians became isolated from the mainland. After British settlement, conflict known as the Black War (1820s–1832) and disease killed most of the Aboriginal population, and survivors were removed to Flinders Island. Truganini, who died in 1876, was long wrongly described as the ‘last’ Aboriginal Tasmanian; thousands of palawa descendants live in Tasmania today."],["Convict colony","Tasmania, then called Van Diemen’s Land, received about 75,000 convicts between 1803 and 1853, when transportation ended. The Port Arthur penal settlement, founded in 1830, is now a historic site and part of the Australian Convict Sites World Heritage listing. The colony was renamed Tasmania in 1856."],["Wilderness and conservation","In the early 1980s, a campaign to stop a dam on the Franklin River became one of Australia’s most famous environmental battles; the High Court ruled for the river’s protection in 1983. The movement helped give rise to the world’s first green political party, the United Tasmania Group, in 1972."],["Wildlife","The Tasmanian devil, a fierce-sounding marsupial carnivore about the size of a small dog, survives in the wild only in Tasmania. Since 1996, a contagious facial tumor disease has killed most devils, and conservationists breed healthy populations. The last known thylacine died in Hobart Zoo on 7 September 1936."]],
k:[["Capital","Hobart"],["Population","About 575,000"],["Distance from mainland","About 240 km across Bass Strait"],["Protected land","About 40%"],["European arrival","Abel Tasman, 1642"],["Famous animal","Tasmanian devil"]],
d:[["Ancient times","Aboriginal people live in Tasmania for at least 40,000 years"],["1642","Abel Tasman reaches the island"],["1803","British colony founded"],["1830","Port Arthur penal settlement founded"],["1853","Transportation of convicts ends"],["1856","Van Diemen’s Land renamed Tasmania"],["1936","Last known thylacine dies"],["1982","Tasmanian Wilderness World Heritage listing"]],
s2:[["The thylacine","The thylacine, a striped, dog-like marsupial, was hunted to extinction after farmers blamed it for killing sheep. Unconfirmed sightings are still reported, and scientists have studied its preserved DNA, with some companies even proposing to bring it back."],["Cleanest air","The Cape Grim station on Tasmania’s northwest coast measures some of the cleanest air on Earth, because winds arrive after crossing thousands of kilometers of Southern Ocean. Its records are used to track changes in the global atmosphere."]],
sp:["MONA","The Museum of Old and New Art (MONA) in Hobart, opened in 2011 by gambler and collector David Walsh, is built into a riverside cliff and displays provocative ancient and modern art. It became one of Australia’s most talked-about museums."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that carries its young in a pouch"],["Convict","A person serving a prison sentence, often sent to a colony"],["Penal settlement","A colony for prisoners"],["Wilderness","Wild, natural land largely untouched by people"]],
f:["Tasmania was first called Van Diemen’s Land.","Wild Tasmanian devils live only in Tasmania.","The last known thylacine died in 1936."],
fun:"Tasmanian devils got their name from early European settlers who heard their loud, eerie screeches at night."});
